# Overview
#
# A version named relative to the writer's present (`in older versions`,
# `newer kernels`, `a recent version of client-go`) leaves the reader to
# guess where the line falls, and the line moves with every release. The
# fix names the version (`before v1.24`, `kernels 5.8 and later`). The
# rule sits at Recommended, because a phrase can also mean any earlier
# version or have no version to name.
#
# Expression
#
# `older versions` and its kin count only after `in` or before `of`.
# `recent` and `newer` before `version` or `release` count only after
# `a` or `the`. `modern`, `newer`, `older`, and `recent` count before a
# noun for hardware or platforms (`modern CPUs`, `older kernels`).
#
# False Positives
#
# A bare `older versions` often means any earlier version, so it stays
# out.
#
# Related Rules
#
# ProseDatedClaim reports a claim dated to the present, and ProseRoadmap
# reports `in a future release`.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 16 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 13 of 16 left the
# version to the reader. The misfires were a generic `earlier versions
# of an API`, `older versions of Kubernetes` beside words that define
# them, and `modern CPUs`, which has no version to name.
expr when [older, earlier, newer, recent, prior, previous]
expr main [
    (in @when [versions, releases]),
    ([a, the] [recent, newer] [version, release]),
    ([newer, older, recent, modern] [kernels, browsers, CPUs, processors, distributions, compilers]),
    ([older, newer, earlier, recent] versions of)
]
let message "Relative version. Name the version, such as `before v1.24` or `5.8 and later`."
let description "Flags `in older versions`, `newer kernels`, and similar phrases that name a version relative to the present."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "Before v1.24, the flag defaulted to false."
allows "Kernels 5.8 and later support the option."
allows "Roll back to the previous version of the Deployment."
